CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> In Class 6B, the teacher asked everyone to decide the date of the annual picnic. All 40 students raised their hands to vote and the majority date was chosen. Later, the class elected Ananya as the monitor to speak for the class in the school council. "
   "(i) Which method of taking a decision is shown by the picnic vote? (ii) Which method is shown by electing Ananya? (iii) What is a person elected to speak for others called? (iv) Why can large countries like India not use only the first method?",
   "(i) Direct democracy (1). (ii) Representative democracy (1). (iii) A representative (1). (iv) Crores of people cannot meet and decide every matter together (1); so they elect representatives such as MPs and MLAs to take decisions for them (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> A State Assembly passes a law to build a new hospital. The State government's departments hire staff and construct it. When a contractor breaks the rules, a citizen goes to court, and the court orders the contractor to correct the work. "
   "(i) Which organ passed the law? (ii) Which organ carried out the construction work? (iii) Which organ decided the dispute? (iv) How does this show that the three organs check each other?",
   "(i) Legislature (1). (ii) Executive (1). (iii) Judiciary (1). (iv) Each organ has its own role (1); the court can correct the executive's wrong action, and the executive must act within laws made by the legislature (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> A village in Rajasthan has a problem: its road is damaged. The Gram Panchayat repairs it. The district's highway is repaired by the State government, and a national highway connecting the State to another is handled by the Central government. "
   "(i) Name the three levels of government in India. (ii) Which level handles defence? (iii) Which level looks after the village road in the case? (iv) Why is government divided into levels?",
   "(i) Local, State and Central (1). (ii) Central government (1). (iii) Local government (Gram Panchayat) (1). (iv) Different problems need attention at different scales (1); sharing powers lets decisions be taken closer to the people affected (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

